@startuml local-dev-sandbox
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BackgroundColor<<host>> #E0F2FE
  BackgroundColor<<container>> #F3F4F6
}

title MeshCo — Local developer sandbox (per engineer laptop)

rectangle "Developer laptop" <<host>> {

  rectangle "docker compose: meshco-dev" {
    rectangle "MinIO\n(S3-compatible, single node)" <<container>> as MinIO
    rectangle "Apache Polaris\n(metastore, single node)" <<container>> as Polaris
    rectangle "DuckDB (CLI)\nfor ad-hoc lakehouse queries" <<container>> as DuckDB
    rectangle "dbt Core\n(CLI on host)" <<container>> as DBT
    rectangle "Unity Catalog OSS\n(discovery preview)" <<container>> as Unity
  }

  rectangle "Python venv (uv)" <<host>> {
    rectangle "skhema CLI" as Skhema
    rectangle "gnosis CLI" as Gnosis
  }

  rectangle "Git working copy\n~/meshco/" <<host>> as Git

}

rectangle "GitHub Actions (remote)\n—  full-scale CI on push  —" <<host>> as CI

DBT --> MinIO : Reads + writes bronze/silver/gold
DBT --> Polaris : Register tables
DuckDB --> MinIO
DuckDB --> Polaris : Resolve table names
Unity --> Polaris : Pulls metadata
Skhema --> Git : Reads clients/<name>/
Gnosis --> Git : Reads ontology/
Git ..> CI : push → contract validation + full DSL validate

note right of MinIO
  Uses the same Iceberg format
  as prod. The only difference
  is storage backend (MinIO
  vs S3) and metastore
  (Polaris vs Glue) — both
  Iceberg-compatible.
end note

@enduml
